    "word_count": 50,
    "html": "<p>John Albert Sobrato is an American property developer who built a Silicon Valley real estate fortune. He began selling homes while at Santa Clara University, then developed industrial buildings with his mother and business partners. His Sobrato Organization expanded into offices and apartments while remaining owned entirely by his family.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "A business park inherited by John Sobrato's family became a home for local charities. The Silicon Valley developer helped convert the Milpitas property into free offices for nonprofit organisations, easing one of their recurring costs. The family extended the approach to other sites and made meeting rooms available to groups beyond the resident tenants.\n\nSobrato also backed grants that charities could spend on their general operations. He linked renewed support to their ability to attract additional donations, encouraging a broader funding base. His grandchildren's trusts required them to donate an equivalent amount to charity when receiving distributions, with each recipient choosing the causes.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 95,
      "band": "very_high",
      "reason": "First-person interview directly explains property conversion, grant matching and family trust arrangements. Historical wording avoids claiming unchanged terms."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S4"
    ],
    "word_count": 103,
    "html": "<p>A business park inherited by John Sobrato's family became a home for local charities. The Silicon Valley developer helped convert the Milpitas property into free offices for nonprofit organisations, easing one of their recurring costs. The family extended the approach to other sites and made meeting rooms available to groups beyond the resident tenants.</p>\r\n<p>Sobrato also backed grants that charities could spend on their general operations. He linked renewed support to their ability to attract additional donations, encouraging a broader funding base. His grandchildren's trusts required them to donate an equivalent amount to charity when receiving distributions, with each recipient choosing the causes.</p>\r\n"
  },
